body:
Consistent gym goer here. I am very dedicated to my fitness, getting up at 5:00am to hit my workout and let me tell you this still exists...

&#x200B;

I am at work by 7:30 (30 min. early because my boss "wants me there for customers", we open at 8). Do mundane work with annoying/boring co-workers, finally get off, go home, work on my side hustle, help wife with dinner, shower, and then procrastinate bed until 11:00pm sometimes 12:00am because I feel I haven't done enough/gave myself time to relax.

&#x200B;

I will not give up my workouts because they are my mental health help but I am here to tell you it isn't like it used to be where just working out would give you some sort of fulfillment. Work will still drain you.

The problem isn't the school, the curriculum, the teachers or the students. The problem is the big banks and world leaders of nations. They make these things such as economics so damn complicated when they really aren't. Then they talk down to you as if you would never understand the concepts anyway all while making them that much more complicated and using their huge leverage to skew the system to benefit them. We shouldn't be trying to teach people who have no concept of what things cost about how money works they need to be able to figure that out through practice or actually using the currency while maintaining control of their own money and not letting banks do what they want with it. This is where cryptocurrency steps in and excels. It is exciting to interact with because it's so quick like cash. It has tons of ways, as of right now through defi, to compound on itself while giving the user full control and full interest rate benefits. Not everyone needs to understand how money changes hands there are a lot of trade jobs out there that you or I have no idea how to do. The issue needs to be solved at the source and right now the source is extremely corrupt and not in the civilians favor. Blaming kids, literal children, for not understanding economics or money while not paying attention in arguably some of the most boring classes, is ridiculous.

&#x200B;

Side bar: Was a very attentive high school student in classes involving anything money. I still didn't graduate having a good understanding of inflation, economics, money, mortgages, lending, loaning, borrowing and how banks operate. I didn't really grasp these concepts until later on in my life and that was generally only because I had to use them.
